WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:04.010
Phillip: It’s inevitable that sometimes
an organisation and the people in it,

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.439
will have different values and attitudes.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:11.399
When this happens, it can be difficult to
find the best solution for everyone, after

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:16.119
all everyone in the organisation is expected
to observe the organisation’s values.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.180
Maddy: As a team leader, you might
find yourself in this situation and

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.760
that your loyalties are conflicted.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.620
On one hand, it is important to keep the
organisation united and effective, but on the

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:34.540
other hand, people in the organisation have the
right to express their own opinions, including

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.950
disagreements with organisational values.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.482
If you tell your team that you
won’t tolerate disagreement with the

00:00:40.482 --> 00:00:45.680
organisational values, that won’t encourage
them to think freely and innovatively.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.429
But conversely, if you allow people to
disregard what the organisation expects

00:00:50.429 --> 00:00:55.020
from them, this will damage the work
you need to do and your team’s cohesion.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:59.890
Phillip: When a clash emerges,
people pull in different directions.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:04.640
Whatever the clash is about, the fact
that you’ve lost that unity, means that

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.500
things are going to start going wrong.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:12.070
You no longer have a single clear pathway
to achieve your goals, and people will start

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:16.890
doing things in different ways, causing
confusion, which can have ‘knock on’ effects.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:21.229
Targets may be missed and
resentments between people can grow.

00:01:22.059 --> 00:01:24.900
Maddy: So, let’s have a
look at an example of this:

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.139
Suppose a new person starts in sales.

00:01:28.449 --> 00:01:32.620
They have a great track record and
lots of experience and they really

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:34.190
want to make a good first impression.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:40.549
The company really values honesty and building
long-term relationships, having a small

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:44.969
number of loyal clients they can rely on
rather than the uncertainty of a working with

00:01:44.969 --> 00:01:47.279
a lot of unknown clients on small projects.

00:01:48.109 --> 00:01:53.210
The new person, however, has different values,
they believe in getting as big a roster

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:57.940
of clients as possible, even if that means
stretching the truth about what can be delivered.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:03.419
Although they would never tell an outright
lie, they believe that being a bit economical

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:08.330
with the truth and making the occasional
exaggeration, is just good sales strategy.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:15.240
Phillip: What we see here is a conflict in
individual values and organisational expectations,

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.079
and this could cause resentment from other
members of the sales team if they perceive the

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.410
new person to be breaking the rules they follow.

00:02:23.199 --> 00:02:28.040
This could also cause big problems with
production if they introduce far more clients

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:30.360
than the organisation is set up to expect.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:35.910
In a word, this could cause problems,
which in turn could cause more problems…

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:38.399
This shows a clash in values.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:43.900
The new person’s values, focus on bringing
in as many clients as possible, while the

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:48.280
organisation’s, are focused on building
strong relationships with a small number

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:51.230
of clients, and these are incompatible.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:57.230
Maddy: As a team leader, in a situation like
this, you’d need to make sure the organisation’s

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:01.790
values are being clearly communicated to
everyone in the team from the beginning.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:06.590
You might want to consider sending out
emails explaining what the values are and

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:11.670
why they matter, putting posters up about the
values, or giving presentations about them.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:16.969
This will help to keep people on track and stop
conflicts from happening in the first place.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.510
Phillip: Ignoring the problem
won’t help resolve it.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:25.240
Instead, you should start by talking to the
people involved, and encouraging them to

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:30.310
express what their values are whilst reminding
them what the organisation expects from them.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:34.969
You should also make sure you remind
them why these values exist, and

00:03:34.969 --> 00:03:36.859
what they’re intended to achieve.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.020
This will help them understand
that the values aren’t arbitrary.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:46.089
Maddy: You should also look to see if you
can find some common ground, and if there

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:48.399
are principles, they may be willing to adapt.

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:52.840
It’s always best to try and find a
compromise if you can, but not at the

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.610
expense of the organisational values.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:59.600
Phillip: You may even end up having to find a
different role for the person in the organisation,

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:01.590
where their values don’t cause conflict.

00:04:02.139 --> 00:04:07.079
If we consider our example, in this case
maybe the new person in sales, would be

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:08.930
better suited to working in marketing.

00:04:09.719 --> 00:04:14.570
Maddy: And if all else fails and the problem
doesn’t go away, you may have no other option

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:19.429
than to formally escalate it as a disciplinary
matter, but always make sure you follow

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:22.549
your organisation’s policies for doing this.

